  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nBroad mid-level ridging holds across the Southern CONUS into the \nCentral Plains early this morning as Hurricane Isaias approaches the \nGulf Coast. Temperatures at 2 AM are only a few degrees below normal \nhighs for this time of year, and will only cool into the low to mid \n60s by daybreak. A modest tightening of the pressure gradient will \nallow for slightly deeper mixing today than yesterday, resulting in \nhighs near-record values in the upper 80s to low 90s.\n\nAbove-normal warmth and dry conditions remain the theme into early \nnext week as the mid-level ridge holds across the region, sandwiched \nbetween the remnants of Isaias as it lifts north and a potent trough \nacross the western US. Winds do increase this weekend with gusts of \n20-35 MPH expected, highest across north central Kansas. The pattern \nis still progged to change next week as the aforementioned western \ntrough ejects across the Plains, bringing tropical moisture from \ncurrent Hurricane Simon and Tropical Storm Rachel along with it. \nGuidance continues to vary in speed of this trough, although most \nensemble members bring a front into the area by Tuesday. A weak \nperturbation in the southwest flow along with frontal convergence \ncould be sufficient for showers and storms to form during the day \nTuesday, but the highest chances come as the trough approaches \nTuesday night through Wednesday night. PWATs are progged to be near \nclimatological maximums for mid October, so there is at least some \npotential for locally heavy rainfall. Effective shear of around 40 \nkts coupled with modest instability could support a few strong \nstorms as well. There is plenty of time to iron out the rainfall\nand severe weather details in the days ahead,so stayed tuned. \nTemperatures trend back near-normal behind a cold front for the \nend of next week.",
